    {
      "id": "NdePGDFpiRQLi1KSn9f86",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 24283.180746889924,
      "y": 46112.498234666826,
      "width": 855.371337890625,
      "height": 175,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#b2f2bb",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "A5bUPhBSFzz9-CoSwAifb",
        "DE77SXX6KpuR19jWmqmrh",
        "K0x2oBap-Gtcf4fdB1VNH",
        "yH_6nJM9zWQ3lXhNFvPT8",
        "VUC7AFic4neijwuood9F7",
        "avU27ZgV4Ni1Ag53chbX6"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bdS",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 823287825,
      "version": 550,
      "versionNonce": 381194675,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1779808166767,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "There are 4 different ways to implement Proximity search algorithm\n1. Quad tree\n2. Geo Hash\n3. Postgress with GIS Extension\n4. Elastic Search",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "There are 4 different ways to implement Proximity search algorithm\n1. Quad tree\n2. Geo Hash\n3. Postgress with GIS Extension\n4. Elastic Search",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "6pQu_PELN-ZDn-EESMerj",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 24283.180746889913,
      "y": 46341.06966323826,
      "width": 1261.2869873046875,
      "height": 1260,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#b2f2bb",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "A5bUPhBSFzz9-CoSwAifb",
        "DE77SXX6KpuR19jWmqmrh",
        "K0x2oBap-Gtcf4fdB1VNH",
        "yH_6nJM9zWQ3lXhNFvPT8",
        "VUC7AFic4neijwuood9F7",
        "avU27ZgV4Ni1Ag53chbX6"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bdT",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 502199761,
      "version": 1580,
      "versionNonce": 1008260861,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1779808166767,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Quad tree\n- easy to explain \n- very very very difficult to implement and maintain\n- quad means 4 and tree means tree ds\n\n\nIn quad tree, we divide the whole world map into 4 parts.\nEach part is again divided into 4 smaller parts.\nThis division continues until we get small areas like 200m or 500m.\nor there is no just hotel/rider/restaurant in that area.\n\nthis will be huge quad tree. like thousand and thousands of levels. \nwhere there will be lots and lots of hotels/drivers/restaurants.\nand there will be very less levels in areas like river and oceans.\n\ncons:\ndata is not uniformly distributed. so quad tree will not be balanced tree. like \nin ocean area there cant be any hotel/rider/restaurant. \nso dividing of this quadrant is not uniform\nsearching of hotes/riders/restaurants is literally a time consuming\n\nNow suppose user is standing at one location and wants all restaurants within 5 km radius.\nIf the user’s own small box does not have enough restaurants, then we cannot stop there.\nWe have to go to the parent box.\nThen we have to check its child boxes.\nIf still we do not get all restaurants in 5 km radius, then again we have to go to the parent of parent.\nThen again we have to check many child boxes.\nSo for one search, we may need to:\n    go up in the tree,\n    check parent node,\n    check child nodes,\n    check neighbouring boxes,\n    and repeat this again and again.\nThat is why search becomes time consuming.\n\nsince this is difficult to implement -- avoid in system design interview.",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Quad tree\n- easy to explain \n- very very very difficult to implement and maintain\n- quad means 4 and tree means tree ds\n\n\nIn quad tree, we divide the whole world map into 4 parts.\nEach part is again divided into 4 smaller parts.\nThis division continues until we get small areas like 200m or 500m.\nor there is no just hotel/rider/restaurant in that area.\n\nthis will be huge quad tree. like thousand and thousands of levels. \nwhere there will be lots and lots of hotels/drivers/restaurants.\nand there will be very less levels in areas like river and oceans.\n\ncons:\ndata is not uniformly distributed. so quad tree will not be balanced tree. like \nin ocean area there cant be any hotel/rider/restaurant. \nso dividing of this quadrant is not uniform\nsearching of hotes/riders/restaurants is literally a time consuming\n\nNow suppose user is standing at one location and wants all restaurants within 5 km radius.\nIf the user’s own small box does not have enough restaurants, then we cannot stop there.\nWe have to go to the parent box.\nThen we have to check its child boxes.\nIf still we do not get all restaurants in 5 km radius, then again we have to go to the parent of parent.\nThen again we have to check many child boxes.\nSo for one search, we may need to:\n    go up in the tree,\n    check parent node,\n    check child nodes,\n    check neighbouring boxes,\n    and repeat this again and again.\nThat is why search becomes time consuming.\n\nsince this is difficult to implement -- avoid in system design interview.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
