    {
      "id": "Ohwn2T489-yJcnyGwv4Ti",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 21677.0577306298,
      "y": 42624.41909209541,
      "width": 2177.095540364582,
      "height": 14140,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "D4o8Bu_Pn6twGb7gjvnCC",
        "DE77SXX6KpuR19jWmqmrh",
        "K0x2oBap-Gtcf4fdB1VNH",
        "yH_6nJM9zWQ3lXhNFvPT8",
        "VUC7AFic4neijwuood9F7",
        "avU27ZgV4Ni1Ag53chbX6"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "beI",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2113928497,
      "version": 9398,
      "versionNonce": 1434834931,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "1Z52lIgTy884E5p9vDBFO",
          "type": "arrow"
        },
        {
          "id": "uQQ7Yh2J5y07kABjDigU8",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1779808166767,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "User svc \n\nuser svc is responsible for:\n    user registration\n    login/logout\n    authentication\nuser requests first come to API gateway, then LB routes request to user svc instances.\nduring signup:\n    user svc validates email/password\n    password is hashed using bcrypt\n    user details are stored in user DB\nduring login:\n    user svc validates credentials\n    generates JWT access token + refresh token\n    token is returned to client\nJWT contains:\n    userId\n    expiry\n    roles\nclient uses this JWT token while calling other services like:\n    search svc\n    booking svc \n    property svc\nother services validate JWT locally instead of calling user svc again and again.\nuser DB is sharded using hash(email_id).\nconsistent hashing is used to distribute load and minimize data movement during re-sharding.\n\n\n\n\n\nproperty svc:\n\nproperty svc is responsible for:\n    * creating property/listing\n    * updating property details\n    * managing price\n    * managing availability\n    * storing property metadata\n\nproperty owner request first comes to API gateway, then LB routes request to property svc instances.\nproperty images are not stored in property DB.\nimages are stored in object storage like S3. \nproperty DB only stores image URLs and metadata.\nproperty DB can be sharded using propertyId/location\n\n\n\n\n\nSearch svc:\n\nUser can search base on multiple things like : name, location, date range.\nprice of room can vary time to time, baseon seasons. so each room will have diff price in diff timeframe\nmost imp thing is availibilibity of room, when an where and which rooms is available\n\nwe will need to keep track of all of those things. (check schema)\nall hotel related data is related (hotel, rooms, availibilty, price) we will use sql\n \nwe will also have to store lat and long of each hotel, for proximity search like if user ask to get hotels within 5km range.\neach room have diff price in diff time frame - so we will have to keep track of that \nhere its difficult to keep track of availability - coz we cant store like booked dates. \ncoz while searching we hvae to make sure we return all avaialbel hotels withit that timefarme effectively easity and super fsat\nso saving availiibiily of room is quite critical. \n\n2 apprach:\n- store 365 entries in availibility table with stuatus of room for each day of calender year.\n    this is highly efficient for searching;\n    but downside is .. amout of data we are stroing in db is quite huge. if 1 hotel has 300 rooms. each of those will have 365 days entry in availibity table. that horrib le\n    and from non fucntional req we can see that we will be having 1m hotesl.. asuuming each hotel has 100 rooms. its to much of data being stgore in db \n\n- storing in the form of available date range.\n    this eliminates 365 records to very few very verfy very few\n    but downside is... maintianing this schema structue is bit difficualt \n    if customer comes and book for somewehere in betwen and then wewill have to split thi record into 2 recoreds.\n    we will have to split this data in like for couple of days its avaiable then for next couple of days its not and then again couple fof days its availbale. its increases\ncomplexity\n    technically we are modifiying old data which is not prefer in large scale produyction systems\n\nsummary:\nApproach 1 stores one record per room per day.\nIt is very good for searching but storage size becomes huge.\nApproach 2 stores availability as date ranges.\nIt saves storage but update logic becomes complex.\n\n(discuss both apprach with in terviewer and let him select one. \nhere we will go wtih 1st\n\n\nhere hotel images will be stored in object storage like s3 and its url will be stored in db.\n\nso hotels db has 4 relational tables.\nif user search smg. we will have to perform joins betwenn all 4 tables base on user query, title, location, price, availibitlity, size. and then reutrn the restult \nthis will work.. but it would be damm slow and latency would increase.\n\nto optimze this we will be using elastic search.\nsearch svc will query Elasticsearch instead of querying property DB directly.\n\nhere user can search base on name, location, city, date. elastic search can perform well with textual search., like name.\nwhat about date and location?\n\nwhen we need to search baseon location: its all about proximity search. like give me all data within radius of xkm\n\nthere are 4 ways to implement proximity search algorithm\n\n1. we cant use quad tree - coz we are here we are dealing with 1M+ hotel and to build quad tree we need space and space for 1m hotels would be more\n2. same reason why cant we use geo hash\n\nhere we are also having text base search for location and also lat and long  \nso we have to either go with postgress (gis and fuzzystrmatch) or elastic search\n3. we are already using postress db ... we will have to install extention called as gis . we can use this apprach but\n4. we are here dealing with textual search and location base search and for textual we are alreayd using elastirc search. so for locaiton as well we will use same.\n\nquad tree, geo hash dont support textual search\npostgress also need 2 different extension for textual and location base search\n\nelastic search has both inbuilt.\n\n\n\n\n\nElastic Search svc\n\nNow data will be inserted into elastic search using CDC pipeline.\n\nTools like Debezium will be connected to DB binlog / WAL logs.\nDebezium watches DB logs.\n\nWhenever there is any new log / insert / update / delete,\nDebezium captures that change and pushes event into Kafka queue.\n\nConsumer svc consumes those events from Kafka\nand updates Elasticsearch.\n\n\n\n\nproblem is we have multiple interconnected db\nwe cant insert in elastic search directly \n\nwe have 2 apprach for that:\n1. we will have to create service that normalize data before inserting in to elastic searcch\n2. we will not insert unrelated data in elastic serach (like availibitly, price, rooms)\n\nwe will go with 2nd coz: elastic search will return handfull amount of hotels which are near to user locaiotn \nwe can perform joins with price and avalibility if needed in search svc and then RANK the result based on user need and then return result to user in the form of pagination.\nto optimzei joins to query db, we will use redis cache to store price availibnty and rooms\nthat would be optimal way of doing this.\n\n\n\n\n\nReview svc\n\nuser can submit review for that property/hotel.\n\nreview request first comes to API gateway,\nthen LB routes request to review svc instances.\nreview data can be stored in separate review DB.\n\nreview svc can publish event to Kafka\nso search/index service can update average rating in Elasticsearch.\n\n\n\n\n\n\nbooking svc\n\nwhen user clicks book,\nrequest first comes to API gateway,\nthen LB routes request to booking svc instances.\n\nbooking svc creates reservation for that hotel/room/date.\n\nbooking details are stored in BookingDB with RESERVED / PENDING_PAYMENT status.\n\nbooking svc returns:\n    reservationId\n    amount\n\nclient uses this reservationId to initiate payment.\n\nbooking status is not confirmed yet.\nit will be confirmed only after payment success.\n\nNOTE: Client sends an idempotency key with booking request.\n\nThis prevents duplicate booking creation if client retries due to timeout/network issue.\n\nBooking svc stores:\n- idempotencyKey\n- userId\n- bookingId\n- request hash\n\nIf same idempotency key comes again,\nBooking svc returns the same reservationId instead of creating a new booking.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\npayment svc\n\nclient calls payment initiate API with:\n    reservationId\n    amount\n    method\n\npayment svc creates payment request\nand talks to payment gateway.\n\npayment svc returns payment token to client.\n\nclient uses this token to make payment using selected method.\n\nonce payment is done,\npayment gateway calls payment callback API.\n\ncallback contains:\n    paymentId\n    reservationId\n    status\n    transactionId\n    signature\n\npayment svc verifies signature.\n\nif signature is valid and payment is successful:\n    payment svc marks payment as CONFIRMED\n    payment svc publishes event to Kafka\n\nconsumer svc consumes event from Kafka\n    and updates BookingDB status as CONFIRMED.\n    Room Avail svc updates room/property availability as BOOKED after successful booking/payment.\n    Notification svc sends booking confirmation notification to user using (APNs or FCM)\n\nif payment fails:\n    payment svc marks payment as FAILED\n    booking status becomes FAILED\n    room/property availability is not marked as booked.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nExpiry worker\n\nWe also need an expiry worker.\n\nWhen booking svc creates a temporary hold, it stores holdExpiryTime.\n\nIf user does not complete payment within 5 minutes,\nExpiry Worker finds expired PENDING_PAYMENT bookings.\n\nFor expired bookings:\n- booking status becomes EXPIRED\n- availability status changes from HELD back to AVAILABLE\n- Redis lock, if present, expires automatically\n\nThis prevents rooms from being blocked forever.\n\n\n\n\n\n\nBooking info svc\n\nbooking info svc fetches booking history.\n\nfor guest:\n    fetch bookings by userId.\n\nfor host:\n    fetch bookings by hostId/propertyId.\n\nit reads data from BookingDB\nand returns paginated booking list.\n\n\n\n\n\nProblem:\n\nTwo users can try to book the same room for the same date range.\n\nIf both requests come almost at the same time,\nboth can see the room as available.\n\nThen both users may go to payment page.\n\nThis can create double booking.\n\n\n\n\nTo handle concurrent bookings : we use lockings\n\n\nSolution1: Pessimistic locking\n\nIn pessimistic locking, we lock the room/property row before booking.\n\nWhen user tries to book a room,\nbooking svc starts DB transaction.\n\nIt selects availability row using FOR UPDATE.\n\nThis locks that row.\n\nOther users cannot update same room/date row until first transaction is completed.\n\nIf payment/booking succeeds:\n    status becomes BOOKED\n\nIf booking fails:\n    transaction rollback happens\n    lock is released\n\nThis avoids double booking but can reduce performance because other users have to wait.\n\nThis is kind of less concurrent (infact it eliminates concurrency totally - only one user can process at a time and other waits)\nthis is not scalable (if 10 users waits then latency would increase significantly)\n\n\n\n\n\n\nSolution 2: Optimistic locking\n\nIn optimistic locking, we do not lock row initially.\n\nEach availability row has a version number.\n\nWhen user tries to book,\nbooking svc reads availability row with version.\n\nWhile updating booking status,\nit checks same version.\n\nExample:\n    update availability\n    set status = BOOKED, version = version + 1\n    where roomId = r1 and date = d1 and status = AVAILABLE and version = oldVersion\n\nIf update succeeds:\n    booking is confirmed\n\nIf update fails:\n    someone else already booked it\n    user gets booking failed message\n\nThis gives better performance but needs retry/failure handling.\n\nThis handles highly concurrent requests. and data stays consistent. This is scalable and it can handle high and peak traffic effectively\n\n\n\n\n\nSolution 3: Redis distributed locking\n\nBefore redirecting user to payment gateway,\nbooking svc will check room availability again.\n\nIf room is available,\nbooking svc will take Redis lock on that room for that date range.\n\nExample lock key:\nroomId:startDate:endDate\n\nThis lock will be kept for small time, like 5 minutes.\n\nDuring this 5 minutes,\nno other user can book the same room for same date range.\n\nUser will complete payment in this time.\n\nIf payment succeeds:\n    booking is confirmed\n    availability is updated as BOOKED\n    Redis lock is released\n\nIf payment fails or timeout happens:\n    Redis lock expires automatically\n    room becomes available again\n\n\n\n\n\n\nwhich appoach of lockgni to choose you can discuss with interviewer but prefer distributed redis locking or optimistic locking",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "User svc \n\nuser svc is responsible for:\n    user registration\n    login/logout\n    authentication\nuser requests first come to API gateway, then LB routes request to user svc instances.\nduring signup:\n    user svc validates email/password\n    password is hashed using bcrypt\n    user details are stored in user DB\nduring login:\n    user svc validates credentials\n    generates JWT access token + refresh token\n    token is returned to client\nJWT contains:\n    userId\n    expiry\n    roles\nclient uses this JWT token while calling other services like:\n    search svc\n    booking svc \n    property svc\nother services validate JWT locally instead of calling user svc again and again.\nuser DB is sharded using hash(email_id).\nconsistent hashing is used to distribute load and minimize data movement during re-sharding.\n\n\n\n\n\nproperty svc:\n\nproperty svc is responsible for:\n    * creating property/listing\n    * updating property details\n    * managing price\n    * managing availability\n    * storing property metadata\n\nproperty owner request first comes to API gateway, then LB routes request to property svc instances.\nproperty images are not stored in property DB.\nimages are stored in object storage like S3. \nproperty DB only stores image URLs and metadata.\nproperty DB can be sharded using propertyId/location\n\n\n\n\n\nSearch svc:\n\nUser can search base on multiple things like : name, location, date range.\nprice of room can vary time to time, baseon seasons. so each room will have diff price in diff timeframe\nmost imp thing is availibilibity of room, when an where and which rooms is available\n\nwe will need to keep track of all of those things. (check schema)\nall hotel related data is related (hotel, rooms, availibilty, price) we will use sql\n \nwe will also have to store lat and long of each hotel, for proximity search like if user ask to get hotels within 5km range.\neach room have diff price in diff time frame - so we will have to keep track of that \nhere its difficult to keep track of availability - coz we cant store like booked dates. \ncoz while searching we hvae to make sure we return all avaialbel hotels withit that timefarme effectively easity and super fsat\nso saving availiibiily of room is quite critical. \n\n2 apprach:\n- store 365 entries in availibility table with stuatus of room for each day of calender year.\n    this is highly efficient for searching;\n    but downside is .. amout of data we are stroing in db is quite huge. if 1 hotel has 300 rooms. each of those will have 365 days entry in availibity table. that horrib le\n    and from non fucntional req we can see that we will be having 1m hotesl.. asuuming each hotel has 100 rooms. its to much of data being stgore in db \n\n- storing in the form of available date range.\n    this eliminates 365 records to very few very verfy very few\n    but downside is... maintianing this schema structue is bit difficualt \n    if customer comes and book for somewehere in betwen and then wewill have to split thi record into 2 recoreds.\n    we will have to split this data in like for couple of days its avaiable then for next couple of days its not and then again couple fof days its availbale. its increases complexity\n    technically we are modifiying old data which is not prefer in large scale produyction systems\n\nsummary:\nApproach 1 stores one record per room per day.\nIt is very good for searching but storage size becomes huge.\nApproach 2 stores availability as date ranges.\nIt saves storage but update logic becomes complex.\n\n(discuss both apprach with in terviewer and let him select one. \nhere we will go wtih 1st\n\n\nhere hotel images will be stored in object storage like s3 and its url will be stored in db.\n\nso hotels db has 4 relational tables.\nif user search smg. we will have to perform joins betwenn all 4 tables base on user query, title, location, price, availibitlity, size. and then reutrn the restult \nthis will work.. but it would be damm slow and latency would increase.\n\nto optimze this we will be using elastic search.\nsearch svc will query Elasticsearch instead of querying property DB directly.\n\nhere user can search base on name, location, city, date. elastic search can perform well with textual search., like name.\nwhat about date and location?\n\nwhen we need to search baseon location: its all about proximity search. like give me all data within radius of xkm\n\nthere are 4 ways to implement proximity search algorithm\n\n1. we cant use quad tree - coz we are here we are dealing with 1M+ hotel and to build quad tree we need space and space for 1m hotels would be more\n2. same reason why cant we use geo hash\n\nhere we are also having text base search for location and also lat and long  \nso we have to either go with postgress (gis and fuzzystrmatch) or elastic search\n3. we are already using postress db ... we will have to install extention called as gis . we can use this apprach but\n4. we are here dealing with textual search and location base search and for textual we are alreayd using elastirc search. so for locaiton as well we will use same.\n\nquad tree, geo hash dont support textual search\npostgress also need 2 different extension for textual and location base search\n\nelastic search has both inbuilt.\n\n\n\n\n\nElastic Search svc\n\nNow data will be inserted into elastic search using CDC pipeline.\n\nTools like Debezium will be connected to DB binlog / WAL logs.\nDebezium watches DB logs.\n\nWhenever there is any new log / insert / update / delete,\nDebezium captures that change and pushes event into Kafka queue.\n\nConsumer svc consumes those events from Kafka\nand updates Elasticsearch.\n\n\n\n\nproblem is we have multiple interconnected db\nwe cant insert in elastic search directly \n\nwe have 2 apprach for that:\n1. we will have to create service that normalize data before inserting in to elastic searcch\n2. we will not insert unrelated data in elastic serach (like availibitly, price, rooms)\n\nwe will go with 2nd coz: elastic search will return handfull amount of hotels which are near to user locaiotn \nwe can perform joins with price and avalibility if needed in search svc and then RANK the result based on user need and then return result to user in the form of pagination.\nto optimzei joins to query db, we will use redis cache to store price availibnty and rooms\nthat would be optimal way of doing this.\n\n\n\n\n\nReview svc\n\nuser can submit review for that property/hotel.\n\nreview request first comes to API gateway,\nthen LB routes request to review svc instances.\nreview data can be stored in separate review DB.\n\nreview svc can publish event to Kafka\nso search/index service can update average rating in Elasticsearch.\n\n\n\n\n\n\nbooking svc\n\nwhen user clicks book,\nrequest first comes to API gateway,\nthen LB routes request to booking svc instances.\n\nbooking svc creates reservation for that hotel/room/date.\n\nbooking details are stored in BookingDB with RESERVED / PENDING_PAYMENT status.\n\nbooking svc returns:\n    reservationId\n    amount\n\nclient uses this reservationId to initiate payment.\n\nbooking status is not confirmed yet.\nit will be confirmed only after payment success.\n\nNOTE: Client sends an idempotency key with booking request.\n\nThis prevents duplicate booking creation if client retries due to timeout/network issue.\n\nBooking svc stores:\n- idempotencyKey\n- userId\n- bookingId\n- request hash\n\nIf same idempotency key comes again,\nBooking svc returns the same reservationId instead of creating a new booking.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\npayment svc\n\nclient calls payment initiate API with:\n    reservationId\n    amount\n    method\n\npayment svc creates payment request\nand talks to payment gateway.\n\npayment svc returns payment token to client.\n\nclient uses this token to make payment using selected method.\n\nonce payment is done,\npayment gateway calls payment callback API.\n\ncallback contains:\n    paymentId\n    reservationId\n    status\n    transactionId\n    signature\n\npayment svc verifies signature.\n\nif signature is valid and payment is successful:\n    payment svc marks payment as CONFIRMED\n    payment svc publishes event to Kafka\n\nconsumer svc consumes event from Kafka\n    and updates BookingDB status as CONFIRMED.\n    Room Avail svc updates room/property availability as BOOKED after successful booking/payment.\n    Notification svc sends booking confirmation notification to user using (APNs or FCM)\n\nif payment fails:\n    payment svc marks payment as FAILED\n    booking status becomes FAILED\n    room/property availability is not marked as booked.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nExpiry worker\n\nWe also need an expiry worker.\n\nWhen booking svc creates a temporary hold, it stores holdExpiryTime.\n\nIf user does not complete payment within 5 minutes,\nExpiry Worker finds expired PENDING_PAYMENT bookings.\n\nFor expired bookings:\n- booking status becomes EXPIRED\n- availability status changes from HELD back to AVAILABLE\n- Redis lock, if present, expires automatically\n\nThis prevents rooms from being blocked forever.\n\n\n\n\n\n\nBooking info svc\n\nbooking info svc fetches booking history.\n\nfor guest:\n    fetch bookings by userId.\n\nfor host:\n    fetch bookings by hostId/propertyId.\n\nit reads data from BookingDB\nand returns paginated booking list.\n\n\n\n\n\nProblem:\n\nTwo users can try to book the same room for the same date range.\n\nIf both requests come almost at the same time,\nboth can see the room as available.\n\nThen both users may go to payment page.\n\nThis can create double booking.\n\n\n\n\nTo handle concurrent bookings : we use lockings\n\n\nSolution1: Pessimistic locking\n\nIn pessimistic locking, we lock the room/property row before booking.\n\nWhen user tries to book a room,\nbooking svc starts DB transaction.\n\nIt selects availability row using FOR UPDATE.\n\nThis locks that row.\n\nOther users cannot update same room/date row until first transaction is completed.\n\nIf payment/booking succeeds:\n    status becomes BOOKED\n\nIf booking fails:\n    transaction rollback happens\n    lock is released\n\nThis avoids double booking but can reduce performance because other users have to wait.\n\nThis is kind of less concurrent (infact it eliminates concurrency totally - only one user can process at a time and other waits)\nthis is not scalable (if 10 users waits then latency would increase significantly)\n\n\n\n\n\n\nSolution 2: Optimistic locking\n\nIn optimistic locking, we do not lock row initially.\n\nEach availability row has a version number.\n\nWhen user tries to book,\nbooking svc reads availability row with version.\n\nWhile updating booking status,\nit checks same version.\n\nExample:\n    update availability\n    set status = BOOKED, version = version + 1\n    where roomId = r1 and date = d1 and status = AVAILABLE and version = oldVersion\n\nIf update succeeds:\n    booking is confirmed\n\nIf update fails:\n    someone else already booked it\n    user gets booking failed message\n\nThis gives better performance but needs retry/failure handling.\n\nThis handles highly concurrent requests. and data stays consistent. This is scalable and it can handle high and peak traffic effectively\n\n\n\n\n\nSolution 3: Redis distributed locking\n\nBefore redirecting user to payment gateway,\nbooking svc will check room availability again.\n\nIf room is available,\nbooking svc will take Redis lock on that room for that date range.\n\nExample lock key:\nroomId:startDate:endDate\n\nThis lock will be kept for small time, like 5 minutes.\n\nDuring this 5 minutes,\nno other user can book the same room for same date range.\n\nUser will complete payment in this time.\n\nIf payment succeeds:\n    booking is confirmed\n    availability is updated as BOOKED\n    Redis lock is released\n\nIf payment fails or timeout happens:\n    Redis lock expires automatically\n    room becomes available again\n\n\n\n\n\n\nwhich appoach of lockgni to choose you can discuss with interviewer but prefer distributed redis locking or optimistic locking",
